00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
The stock has now gone against me.

159
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
It's down 10% from where you bought it.

160
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:59,000
And asking yourself the question, what on
earth should I do?

161
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:04,000
Well, we looked at preventative risk
management situations in earlier videos.

162
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:09,000
You can always have some sort of stop loss
in place, obviously.

163
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:15,000
But remember, you are running a long short
portfolio and there will be correlation in
your portfolio.

164
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:23,000
So there is no one size fits all rule book
that applies to all situations all the
time.

165
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:27,000
Every situation is dependent on its own
merits.

166
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,000
So remember, there is no rule book here.

167
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:43,000
But one thing is for certain, just using
the excuse like it's not working because
the technicals didn't play out is really
just a dumb excuse.

168
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:53,000
It's probably not going up because you've
actually missed something fundamental and
or catalyst driven.

169
00:21:53,000 --> 00:22:01,000
So it's probably not going up because of a
lack of catalysts or you've missed
something fundamentally.

170
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:17,000
And in reality, there's multiple options
in how you treat this type of situation
versus just the binary make $3 or lose $1,
the three to one ratio on a trade.

171
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:23,000
The options in the way that you treat a
position like this are literally endless.

172
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:31,000
And what you have to remember as well is
that it's just one position in the context
of your entire portfolio.

173
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:38,000
The outcome of your portfolio is way more
important than the outcome of just one
position.

174
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:47,000
And this is why you have to eliminate all
emotion and stay objective all the time on
any position.

175
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:53,000
Now, something else that we have to
consider is something that we call a
stomach churner.

176
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:59,000
So when you've lost so much money that you
are actually sick to your stomach.

177
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:05,000
So this is what we call in trading and
portfolio management, the catastrophic
loss.

178
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:14,000
So you just log in one day into your
trading account and your account is just
down a massive amount of money.

179
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:18,000
Now, this can only happen for a few
reasons.

180
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:27,000
First of all, it could be a situation that
you just have far too much concentrated
risk in one situation.

181
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,000
So you're actually not diverse enough in
your portfolio.

182
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:35,000
So you didn't commit to actually building
a portfolio.

183
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:47,000
Secondly, it could be the case that
everything in your portfolio is just too
highly correlated and you've possibly
topped and tailed the portfolio.

184
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:51,000
So what does that mean in the context of a
long short portfolio?

185
00:23:51,000 --> 00:24:07,000
Well, it could just mean that if the
market goes up 10% and all of your longs
and shorts went up between 10% and 20%
that you had hard stop losses on all of
your shorts and you got stopped out on all
of your shorts on the way up.

186
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,000
And now you're just naked long.

187
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:13,000
So you only have your long positions
remaining.

188
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:17,000
And then the market reverses and all of
your longs go down.

189
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:25,000
This means you've booked realized losses
on all of your shorts and realized losses
on all of your longs.

190
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
So essentially, what does that mean?

191
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:31,000
It means you've been hit with a double
whammy.

192
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
In that situation, everything was moving
in the same direction.

193
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:43,000
You probably had decent diversity in your
portfolio, but you had quite a bit of
correlation in your portfolio.

194
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,000
Volatility comes into the market.

195
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
All your stocks go up.

196
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:53,000
You get stopped out on all your shorts and
then all your stocks go down and you get
stopped out on all your longs.

197
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:57,000
And this actually may not be your fault.

198
00:24:57,000 --> 00:25:04,000
Of course, putting hard stop losses on
every single position doesn't really work
in a long short portfolio.

199
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:10,000
You have to have a hybrid approach and
assess every situation by its own
individual merits.

200
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:20,000
And of course, if you did that and
volatility came into the market, you might
be a victim of the double whammy and it
might actually be your fault.

201
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:29,000
But what about the situation where, for
example, you walk in one day and all of
your shorts are up and all of your longs
are down?

202
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:33,000
Well, in that situation, it's just really
bad luck.

203
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:36,000
You've done everything right, but you just
got unlucky.

204
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:43,000
You've basically had what we call a three
or four standard deviation day.

205
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:54,000
Where on the very rare occurrence,
everything in your portfolio or the vast
majority of your portfolio just all goes
against you all at once.

206
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:06,000
So these two situations, the head
scratcher and the stomach churner are good
examples where if you don't get your head
right, you don't get your psychology
right.

207
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:13,000
As an amateur trader, a retail trader
starting out and going into the
implementation phase.

208
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:25,000
If you don't get that right, you can end
up struggling for a very long time, even
though you've got a high level
professional standard systematic process
in place.

209
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:39,000
Even worse, if you continue to have your
head in the wrong place, you have bad
psychology and bad preventative risk
management and the actions that you take
with your reactive risk management.

210
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:42,000
Even worse, you're just going to blow up.

211
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:43,000
Why?

212
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:50,000
Because it's absolutely inevitable that
you're going to have situations like this
all the time.

213
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:59,000
It's inevitable when you're trading with
real money in the markets and building
portfolios that you're going to always
have at any one time.

214
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:04,000
Winners, losers and positions that are
kind of flat and doing nothing.

215
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:16,000
So getting your head right, having the
right mindset and approach and knowing the
most probabilistic outcomes from your
actions is not only key to survival,

216
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:29,000
but also absolutely key to you climbing to
the highest level of the competency
hierarchy, unconscious competence and the
professional trader standard level.

217
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:43,000
So let's think about these things in terms
of getting our head right in terms of
outcomes when we have different situations
happening to positions in our portfolio at
any one time.

218
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:49,000
So there are literally only three outcomes
that can occur with a position.

219
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:55,000
There can't be any other outcome on a
position other than these three outcomes.

220
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:05,000
So straight away, this should give us some
sort of comfort in knowing that there's
absolutely no more outcomes than these
three situations.

221
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:09,000
It's not like trading is astrophysics or
particle physics.

222
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:11,000
It's not rocket science.

223
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:15,000
We've just got to understand it and know
how to deal with it.

224
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
And it's actually beautifully simplistic.

225
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:22,000
So on the slide here, you can see the
three outcomes.

226
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:33,000
And what we've done here is highlighted in
red what retail traders generally do and
highlighted in purple what professional
traders usually do.

227
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:38,000
So firstly, a big loss on a position, a
catastrophic loss.

228
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:44,000
What does a retail trader typically do
when they see a position go against them
wildly?

229
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:51,000
Well, generally speaking, most or all
retail traders will just be very scared.

230
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:52,000
Why?

231
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
Because they just don't know what to do.

232
00:28:54,000 --> 00:29:03,000
And what retail traders typically do in
this situation is they just freeze like a
rabbit in the headlights.

233
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:06,000
So not even taking an action on the
position at all.

234
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:22,000
And therefore pulling from box A and even
worse, trying to add to the position and
average down with a losing position in the
hope that they can get back to break even
because they're so scared and they're so
panicked.

235
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:26,000
With professional traders, this just
doesn't happen.

236
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:27,000
Why?

237
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:41,000
Because we know there's a multitude of
actions that we can take to limit losses
and to control the damage and come out of
the situation doing not too bad out of it.

238
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:52,000
So we would usually have our preventative
risk management practices in place to make
sure that we are protected from a
catastrophic loss.

239
00:29:52,000 --> 00:30:02,000
But also due to experience, having been
involved in some situations like that in
the past, we would have good reactive risk
management in place.

240
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:14,000
And even though there's a multitude of
things that we could do, we just wouldn't
panic and we'd assess all of the actions
that we could take with a clear mind.

241
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,000
We don't panic.

242
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:18,000
We don't get scared.

243
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:22,000
And we just trade based on probabilistic
outcomes.

244
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:37,000
So the catastrophic loss, the stomach
churner for professional traders, we know
there's absolutely no need to ever be
scared or panic because no one single
position can really kill us.

245
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:45,000
We construct our portfolios to absolutely
minimize the probability that a
catastrophic loss can occur.

246
00:30:45,000 --> 00:31:00,000
And for us in the first place, because we
have preventative risk management
practices in place, we know that it's
extremely unlikely that we're going to
have a catastrophic loss in our portfolio
at all.

247
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:15,000
And that if we do have a three or four
standard deviation day where everything
just goes against us because it really is
unavoidable to take away all risk from
your portfolio from a catastrophic loss.

248
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:21,000
We understand that if it happens, it's
definitely down to really bad luck.

249
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:26,000
And even in that situation, we wouldn't
even be scared or panicked.

250
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:30,000
We would just do the right thing to make
sure we're trading tomorrow.

251
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:34,000
And that is just about capital
preservation.

252
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Now, what about situations?

253
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:41,000
So our second outcome here where we're
just wrong on a position.

254
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:55,000
Well, even if it's not a catastrophic loss
at the individual stock level or the
overall portfolio level, retail traders,
if they're wrong on a position, will
generally be scared, confused and
panicked.

255
00:31:55,000 --> 00:32:01,000
Whereas professional traders would just
never panic, even if they're wrong on a
position.

256
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:12,000
We would just engage in the right risk
management practices at the preventative
level, where if we were wrong on a
position, it definitely couldn't hurt us
too much.

257
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:18,000
And also we'd engage in reactive risk
management practices.

258
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:30,000
So taking actions on the position, given
new information in the market and the move
in price and just limit the damage that
the position can do to the outcome of our
portfolio.

259
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:36,000
But the key here is that we just don't
panic.

260
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:38,000
We look at things very clearly for what
they are.

261
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:39,000
We eliminate emotion.

262
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:40,000
We stay objective.

263
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:43,000
And we just do probabilistically the right
thing.

264
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:48,000
Now, what about our third outcome where
we're actually right on a position?

265
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:55,000
Well, retail traders, unfortunately,
engage in euphoria when they're right on a
position.

266
00:32:55,000 --> 00:33:13,000
And even when retail traders generally are
experiencing euphoria because they're
right and they have a winner, something's
moved in their favor and they're making
good money on a position, they are still
essentially scared, confused and panicked.

267
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:27,000
And secondly, because the number that
they're looking at can be meaningful to
them, especially if they've had a lot of
previous losses.

268
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:34,000
So they get scared, confused and panicked
and then engage in box B behavior.

269
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:38,000
So taking profits on a winner too early.

270
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:44,000
With professional traders, even with a
winner, we're never going to be scared,
confused or panicked.

271
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:49,000
We don't, in that situation, engage in
emotion.

272
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:52,000
We never panic and we remain objective.

273
00:33:52,000 --> 00:34:03,000
And similar to when we're wrong on a
position, we take a probabilistic approach
and we take the right actions for the
situation that we are in.

274
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:10,000
So to avoid all of these psychological
problems of being scared, confused and
panicked.

275
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:24,000
And then because of those psychological
problems causing us to do stupid things,
we put measures in place to stop us being
psychologically weak as in human.

276
00:34:24,000 --> 00:34:30,000
Why? Because psychological weakness leads
to bad decisions, which leads to losses.

277
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:42,000
So immediately from the outset, before
going into the implementation phase, you
have to absolutely learn to become
agnostic.

278
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:55,000
You have to be able to learn how to
eliminate emotion from your thought
processes when trading and introduce total
objectivity to your psychology.

279
00:34:55,000 --> 00:35:11,000
So whenever you're struggling with your
psychology and actually before you even go
on to implementation, you should engage in
this psychological meditation because it
will just help you put everything into
perspective.

280
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:16,000
It will get your head in the right place.

281
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:26,000
It will help you eliminate emotion and
stay objective so you engage in good
trading behaviors instead of bad trading
behaviors.

282
00:35:26,000 --> 00:35:38,000
Therefore, in all situations, over time,
increasing your probability of making
money and decreasing the probability of
losing money over time.

283
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:41,000
So just think about the planet.

284
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:42,000
Think about Earth.

285
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:43,000
Think about Earth.

286
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:48,000
It's approximately 4,540,000,000 years
old.

