338
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:46,000
And that all you have to do is take the
right actions for that particular
situation that you are in, in that moment
in time in the history of the Earth.

339
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:52,000
And that being emotional about it just
will not serve you for the optimal
outcome.

340
00:42:52,000 --> 00:43:04,000
So eliminating emotion and staying
objective in all situations in financial
markets is very, very important.

341
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:12,000
And you have to really learn how to
harness this skill set of eliminating
emotion and staying objective all the
time.

342
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:26,000
And if we're going to play this game
called trading the financial markets, we
just have to accept some absolute truths
of reality and stay objective around those
truths.

343
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:42,000
So one of these truths is when we go into
the market and we are potentially buying
or selling a stock, we need to just accept
that we're actually going to be down
instantly.

344
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:45,000
We're going to be losing money straight
away.

345
00:43:45,000 --> 00:43:55,000
That's because when we enter the market,
we are buying on the offer and we are
selling or shorting on the bid side.

346
00:43:55,000 --> 00:44:00,000
And then our P&L is marked on the other
side of the spread.

347
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:17,000
So if the spread in a stock is $25.72 to
$25.73, we are buying at $25.73 or we are
shorting at $25.72.

348
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:26,000
If we are buying or going long at $25.73,
our P&L is now marked at $25.72.

349
00:44:26,000 --> 00:44:34,000
If we are shorting the stock at $25.72,
our P&L is now live and marked at $25.73.

350
00:44:34,000 --> 00:44:37,000
So we just have to accept that it's a fact
of life.

351
00:44:37,000 --> 00:44:42,000
As soon as we take a position, we should
expect to be down.

352
00:44:42,000 --> 00:44:58,000
On top of that, we should always have
realistic expectations that we should
never think to ourselves that we are
actually ever going to pick the bottom
when going long or pick the top when we're
selling out of a long or going short.

353
00:44:58,000 --> 00:45:13,000
We should just accept the reality and the
truth of financial markets that when we
enter a position, we are just going to be
down immediately and we should never
expect that we're going to pick the bottom
or pick the top.

354
00:45:13,000 --> 00:45:21,000
Another truth that we acknowledge is that
our trading account is simply a business.

355
00:45:21,000 --> 00:45:26,000
We expect losses to occur all the time as
well as profits.

356
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:32,000
But overall, we just want our gains to be
higher than our losses.

357
00:45:32,000 --> 00:45:37,000
And this is how our business becomes
profitable.

358
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:51,000
We also admit to ourselves that our trade
ideas, even though we've put a ton of work
into them, they may not work in the
timeframe of 20 to 60 days.

359
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:54,000
And we just don't get emotional about it.

360
00:45:54,000 --> 00:46:07,000
And we just admit that once we have a
position and the position is live, that
it's just a ticker on the screen and a
price on the screen that moves around.

361
00:46:07,000 --> 00:46:13,000
And if it doesn't do what we want it to
do, we don't get angry about it.

362
00:46:13,000 --> 00:46:15,000
We don't get emotional about it.

363
00:46:15,000 --> 00:46:20,000
We don't cry or throw our computer
monitors across the room.

364
00:46:20,000 --> 00:46:32,000
The stock that we have a position in is
just a stock trading over the 20 to 60 day
time period in the four and a half billion
year history of the earth.

365
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:39,000
It's meaningless and there's no reason
ever to be emotional about a position.

366
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:48,000
When we have to deal with a loser, when we
lose on a position, we just accept that
we're not going to get emotional about it.

367
00:46:48,000 --> 00:46:53,000
And we're not going to go back into the
market to fight for our money back.

368
00:46:53,000 --> 00:46:57,000
So we're engaging in what we call revenge
trading.

369
00:46:57,000 --> 00:47:02,000
The stock doesn't care what price you
bought it at, what price you shorted it
at.

370
00:47:02,000 --> 00:47:06,000
The stock doesn't care whether you've made
money or lost money.

371
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:13,000
From the two parties between a human and a
stock, it can only be the human that
cares.

372
00:47:13,000 --> 00:47:22,000
And you're only going to care if you don't
treat your trading account like a business
and you get emotional about a stock
position.

373
00:47:22,000 --> 00:47:26,000
You lose and you try to get revenge on the
stock.

374
00:47:26,000 --> 00:47:29,000
The problem is the stock just doesn't
care.

375
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:39,000
In that situation, it's highly likely that
your emotion is going to lead to really
bad decisions and you're just going to
lose more money.

376
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:50,000
Another truth of financial markets that we
simply just have to deal with is that we
have to understand that we have to take
risk in order to get opportunity.

377
00:47:50,000 --> 00:47:53,000
And we are actually on top of it.

378
00:47:53,000 --> 00:48:00,000
So we understand the risk that we're
taking and the opportunity that this risk
presents.

379
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:01,000
Why?

380
00:48:01,000 --> 00:48:08,000
Because we're intellectually honest with
ourselves about the risk and opportunity
within our time horizon.

381
00:48:08,000 --> 00:48:11,000
And lastly, we have to be greedy.

382
00:48:11,000 --> 00:48:17,000
We have to admit to ourselves that when
