493
01:07:04,000 --> 01:07:13,000
And as we've seen, the differences between
pro traders and retail traders in this
respect are numerous.

494
01:07:13,000 --> 01:07:24,000
And the most startling difference is
always how losers and winners are treated
or traded by professional traders relative
to retail traders.

495
01:07:24,000 --> 01:07:43,000
Obviously, professional traders have got a
lot more experience in how to contain
losses and maximize winners through trial
and error over many years of real trading
with real money in real situations over
and over again on repeat.

496
01:07:43,000 --> 01:07:52,000
And whether it's how we treat losers or
how we treat winners, what we call this in
the industry is staying in motion.

497
01:07:52,000 --> 01:07:59,000
And what we're doing here is that we're
always playing the probabilities, not the
possibilities.

498
01:07:59,000 --> 01:08:13,000
There's a very big difference in financial
markets and outcomes in financial markets
with portfolios and positions between what
is possible and what is actually probable.

499
01:08:13,000 --> 01:08:27,000
And when we pro traders are actually
taking actions on our positions, we are
taking actions based on what is probable,
not what is remotely possible.

500
01:08:27,000 --> 01:08:32,000
And yes, we don't obviously get everything
right all the time with our actions.

501
01:08:32,000 --> 01:08:43,000
But if we're taking a probabilistic
approach over many situations over a long
period of time with our three outcomes,
we're going to get better results.

502
01:08:43,000 --> 01:08:59,000
So if you are going off by yourself,
whether that's outside ITPM or becoming an
institute trader, you've got to understand
to take the approach, the probabilistic
approach, not trading remote
possibilities.

503
01:08:59,000 --> 01:09:04,000
And the difference in psychology is really
twofold.

504
01:09:04,000 --> 01:09:11,000
Firstly, with fundamentals, because we as
pro traders, we do our fundamental
homework.

505
01:09:11,000 --> 01:09:14,000
We know why a stock is moving.

506
01:09:14,000 --> 01:09:23,000
So we know it's because of the
fundamentals and it's not down to
something random like an excuse like the
technicals didn't play out.

507
01:09:23,000 --> 01:09:35,000
So in a winning situation, we are much
more confident to run the position or in a
losing situation, we're much more
confident in staying in motion if
necessary.

508
01:09:35,000 --> 01:09:45,000
Secondly, we just never have a situation
where we're sick to our stomach, where we
have stomach churners and we're just never
married to positions.

509
01:09:45,000 --> 01:09:56,000
Why? Because we know that going into every
position, it doesn't matter what it is,
that we're going to treat all positions in
the same way.

510
01:09:56,000 --> 01:10:07,000
And we just don't get emotional or get
tied to narratives or stories when we're
either up in on a winner or down on a
loser.

511
01:10:07,000 --> 01:10:19,000
So just having the right psychology in
place really helps with your reactive risk
management, even if you're just starting
out and going it alone.

512
01:10:19,000 --> 01:10:32,000
So just make sure that your psychology,
your head is in the right place when you
start trading, when you start implementing
what you've learned in the PTM video
series.

513
01:10:32,000 --> 01:10:39,000
And you're taking the approach of trading
your account as if it's a real business.

514
01:10:39,000 --> 01:10:53,000
You have to make sure that when you go
into this, that you're on the front foot
and that you're willing to stay in motion
on your positions because you are running
a business now.

515
01:10:53,000 --> 01:10:56,000
Your trading account is a business.

516
01:10:56,000 --> 01:11:02,000
You can't be in a situation where you just
stare at positions and do nothing.

517
01:11:02,000 --> 01:11:06,000
You will have to take action on your
positions.

518
01:11:06,000 --> 01:11:12,000
So essentially, you will have to
continually stay in motion.

519
01:11:12,000 --> 01:11:22,000
This is something that is unavoidable in
financial markets and trading financial
markets if you want to get to the
professional level standard of trading.

520
01:11:22,000 --> 01:11:26,000
So how do we actually go about staying in
motion?

521
01:11:26,000 --> 01:11:32,000
Well, assuming that you have good
diversification in your portfolio, good
ideas.

522
01:11:32,000 --> 01:11:38,000
You've got controllable position sizes and
you're executing at decent prices.

523
01:11:38,000 --> 01:11:43,000
How do we actually go about actively
trading a long short portfolio?

524
01:11:43,000 --> 01:11:54,000
Well, again, there's no hard and fast
rules here, but there's just common sense
guidelines and best practices or
parameters that you're probably going to
be adhering to.

525
01:11:54,000 --> 01:11:58,000
So we are operating in the 20 to 60 day
time horizon.

526
01:11:58,000 --> 01:12:12,000
And it really depends on many variables,
but the outcome of a position starts to
become known generally in the first month
when we have the position on.

527
01:12:12,000 --> 01:12:15,000
So around 20 days.

528
01:12:15,000 --> 01:12:19,000
Now, of course, it also depends on the
specific situation.

529
01:12:19,000 --> 01:12:23,000
So it depends on the catalysts in that
situation.

530
01:12:23,000 --> 01:12:34,000
But when things start to play out, the
position is either going to be a clear
loser or a clear winner or it's going to
be somewhere in between.

531
01:12:34,000 --> 01:12:37,000
So what we call a nothing position.

532
01:12:37,000 --> 01:12:52,000
And if you're going to stay in motion
effectively and run your account as a
business, you have to understand that in
our trading statistics, we will always
have an unrealized P&L and a realized P&L.

533
01:12:52,000 --> 01:12:59,000
And in the next section of the PTM video
series, Chris Quill is going to go through
a lot of these trader statistics with you.

534
01:12:59,000 --> 01:13:06,000
But for now, just understand the
difference between unrealized P&L and
realized P&L.

535
01:13:06,000 --> 01:13:15,000
Your unrealized P&L is all of your live
positions in your trading accounts that
are what we call marked to market.

536
01:13:15,000 --> 01:13:21,000
So your P&L is marked at the last price of
each stock that you have a position in.

537
01:13:21,000 --> 01:13:31,000
The point is, is your unrealized P&L is
not real because you still have the
positions on and you haven't taken them
off yet.

538
01:13:31,000 --> 01:13:34,000
When you take them off, they become
realized.

539
01:13:34,000 --> 01:13:39,000
So when you take a position off, your P&L
is crystallized.

540
01:13:39,000 --> 01:13:51,000
So with losers beyond, say, around 40
days, we're probably starting to wonder if
our margin in our trading account is
better spent somewhere else.

541
01:13:51,000 --> 01:14:04,000
So in all probability, we're probably
going to start to take our medicine and
we're going to book some losses or an
overall loss on the position.

542
01:14:04,000 --> 01:14:10,000
And these losses are going to be realized
in our trading account.

543
01:14:10,000 --> 01:14:13,000
So realized losses in our business.

544
01:14:13,000 --> 01:14:18,000
Now, how do we counter that so our
business is being run effectively?

545
01:14:18,000 --> 01:14:41,000
Well, with winners, we are banking profits
along the way into the realized column and
we are keeping the cash register ringing
and we're seeking to keep that realized
column, the dollars in the realized column
going higher and higher all the time,
regardless of the fact that we're also
booking some losses.

546
01:14:41,000 --> 01:14:48,000
So even though we're booking some losses
at the same time, we are seeking to keep
the realized column going up.

547
01:14:48,000 --> 01:15:07,000
And after we've taken profits along the
way, banking those profits along the way
with winners to offset losses, we're
generally seeking to potentially go back
into winners or just move on to the next
trade.

548
01:15:07,000 --> 01:15:12,000
Now, it's important to remember that this
is not an everyday thing.

549
01:15:12,000 --> 01:15:20,000
Typically, what will happen is that your
P&L bookings into the realized column are
going to come in clusters.

550
01:15:20,000 --> 01:15:31,000
And this tends to happen when there's some
volatility around your stocks in your
portfolio are moving around and you'll do
several trades all at once.

551
01:15:31,000 --> 01:15:34,000
This is not an everyday thing.

552
01:15:34,000 --> 01:15:49,000
Now, when you're staying in motion and
you're running your trading business, so
the realized column keeps going higher and
you're banking profits along the way out
of winners to offset losers.

553
01:15:49,000 --> 01:15:56,000
And overall, on a net basis, you're
booking a profit overall because of those
actions.

554
01:15:56,000 --> 01:16:07,000
If the only reason that you've come out of
a winner is to make sure that the realized
dollars are going higher because you're
booking a loss somewhere else.

555
01:16:07,000 --> 01:16:22,000
Of course, it's highly likely that you're
going to go back in at some point to the
winner after making sure that nothing has
changed fundamentally or the fundamentals
have gone in your favor further.

556
01:16:22,000 --> 01:16:39,000
The action of banking profits along the
way to offset losers and more is an action
in the industry that we call trading the
P&L and you want to be very careful not to
trade your P&L too often.

557
01:16:39,000 --> 01:16:46,000
Why? Because again, the stock doesn't care
what price you bought and sold the stock
at.

558
01:16:46,000 --> 01:16:49,000
The stock is just going to do what it
wants to do.

559
01:16:49,000 --> 01:17:02,000
And in a situation where the stock
continuously goes in the direction that
you believe it's going to go in and it's
presented itself as a winning position,
it's more than likely going to continue on
that trend.

560
01:17:02,000 --> 01:17:15,000
So you actually have to trade the stock,
but at the same time, not be reckless that
you're never trading the P&L and not
admitting that you are running a business.

561
01:17:15,000 --> 01:17:28,000
So we are constantly walking a tightrope
between what we call trading the stock and
also trading the account as a business.

562
01:17:28,000 --> 01:17:35,000
And it's a fine balancing act to achieve
in many situations over a long period of
time.

563
01:17:35,000 --> 01:17:40,000
It takes a while to get used to, but
you'll just get it over time with
experience.

564
01:17:40,000 --> 01:18:03,000
So in this quite typical situation where
you do have a winner in your portfolio and
you're banking profits along the way to
offset the dollar losses from a loser and
making sure that overall you are booking a
profit in the realized column, it is quite
likely that you're going to go back in to
the winner.

565
01:18:03,000 --> 01:18:15,000
So you're looking after your business
first and foremost, but then you're also
trading the stock and not missing the
opportunity to make even more money on the
winner.

566
01:18:15,000 --> 01:18:18,000
And of course, you're not in a rush.

567
01:18:18,000 --> 01:18:22,000
You don't have to go back in immediately.

568
01:18:22,000 --> 01:18:25,000
Just be opportunistic.

569
01:18:25,000 --> 01:18:34,000
Now, with nothing positions, those
positions where after a period of time,
they haven't presented themselves as a
clear winner or a clear loser.

570
01:18:34,000 --> 01:18:37,000
They're just hovering around flat.

571
01:18:37,000 --> 01:18:42,000
We can actually be active here as well in
order to protect our downside.

572
01:18:42,000 --> 01:18:48,000
But we do most of the time keep the upside
open just in case.

573
01:18:48,000 --> 01:19:02,000
So in all situations, but in particular on
these nothing positions, try to avoid
trading on noise like a piece of news flow
that really doesn't mean anything for the
stock.

574
01:19:02,000 --> 01:19:07,000
Just take this action solely for risk
management purposes.

575
01:19:07,000 --> 01:19:16,000
If you're taking the action of just
trimming and booking small losses from
what we call a nothing position, it's
totally okay.

576
01:19:16,000 --> 01:19:23,000
You're going to have other positions in
your portfolio that are winners that are
going to offset these small losses.

577
01:19:23,000 --> 01:19:25,000
So don't worry about it.

578
01:19:25,000 --> 01:19:30,000
But at the same time, you don't have to be
extreme in these situations.

579
01:19:30,000 --> 01:19:37,000
You always have the choice of just having
a smaller position in nothing positions.

580
01:19:37,000 --> 01:19:44,000
Never close off all of the upside, the
dollar upside potential of a nothing
position.

581
01:19:44,000 --> 01:19:49,000
Just get to a controllable size and be a
little bit more patient.

582
01:19:49,000 --> 01:20:04,000
Now, when it comes to running your trading
account as a business and going from the
level of conscious competence up to the
professional level of unconscious
competence, there are some absolute do not
actions.

583
01:20:04,000 --> 01:20:10,000
So these are the closest things in terms
of reactive risk management.

584
01:20:10,000 --> 01:20:15,000
These are the closest actions you will
ever get to a rule book.

585
01:20:15,000 --> 01:20:25,000
When it comes to losses and you're
experiencing losses, understand that
you're running a business and you have an
unrealized P&L and a realized P&L.

586
01:20:25,000 --> 01:20:31,000
Just never take the action of only booking
just losses.

587
01:20:31,000 --> 01:20:38,000
Always come out of what we call a booking
flurry, having made realized gains
overall.

588
01:20:38,000 --> 01:20:48,000
And you have to do this even if it means
taking off a large percentage of your
entire trading account.

589
01:20:48,000 --> 01:20:55,000
Because you're running a business, you
have to keep realized gains charging
upwards.

590
01:20:55,000 --> 01:20:59,000
You can always go back into winners.

591
01:20:59,000 --> 01:21:05,000
So just make sure you do not book only big
losses.

592
01:21:05,000 --> 01:21:16,000
Once it's realized P&L, or in this
situation, a very big realized L, it can
never be taken back because it's now
realized.

593
01:21:16,000 --> 01:21:20,000
You have to seek to offset it with
winners.

594
01:21:20,000 --> 01:21:31,000
And if you're trading a spread trade,
don't only book the losing side of a
spread and run the winning side.

595
01:21:31,000 --> 01:21:32,000
Why?

596
01:21:32,000 --> 01:21:33,000
Why?

597
01:21:33,000 --> 01:21:39,000
Because you're making the loss realized
and the winner, the profit is not profit.

598
01:21:39,000 --> 01:21:41,000
It's unrealized.

599
01:21:41,000 --> 01:21:48,000
And you open yourself up to the situation
where you're booking a loss on the losing
side of a spread.

600
01:21:48,000 --> 01:21:51,000
You then run the winning side of a spread.

601
01:21:51,000 --> 01:21:59,000
And then the winning side of the spread
turns into a losing position, which means
you lose money on both sides of a spread.

602
01:21:59,000 --> 01:22:06,000
After you've been in the actual situation
where overall you are up on the spread.

603
01:22:06,000 --> 01:22:16,000
A much better action would be to book both
sides of the spread where you take the
loss and you take the gain.

604
01:22:16,000 --> 01:22:19,000
And overall, on a net basis, you are up.

605
01:22:19,000 --> 01:22:25,000
So your business, which is the realized
P&L, is actually up.

606
01:22:25,000 --> 01:22:29,000
And then you go back into the winning side
of the spread.

607
01:22:29,000 --> 01:22:39,000
So, of course, we try as much as possible
to stick to the principle of trading the
stock and not the P&L.

608
01:22:39,000 --> 01:22:49,000
However, we have to just admit to
ourselves that our trading account is a
business and we need that realized P&L
column always going up on a net basis.

609
01:22:49,000 --> 01:22:53,000
So we don't want to be reckless.

610
01:22:53,000 --> 01:23:01,000
If you just find yourself in a situation
where you're booking big losses over and
over into your realized column.

611
01:23:01,000 --> 01:23:07,000
And you're hoping that your unrealized
winners will pay even more in the future.

612
01:23:07,000 --> 01:23:12,000
You're engaging in bad business practices
for your trading account.

613
01:23:12,000 --> 01:23:17,000
This is a pathway, a road that just leads
to hell.

614
01:23:17,000 --> 01:23:24,000
As a trader portfolio manager, you need to
protect your realized P&L column with your
life.

615
01:23:24,000 --> 01:23:38,000
Now, one of the biggest mistakes that we
see at ITPM is newbie traders, retail
traders, even having taken our programs,
not staying in motion at all.

616
01:23:38,000 --> 01:23:45,000
And or people are choosing to stay in
motion, but they're just doing it really
badly.

617
01:23:45,000 --> 01:23:50,000
So reactive risk management and staying in
motion.

618
01:23:50,000 --> 01:24:01,000
It's really a skill set in itself that you
can only learn by doing it with real
dollars over many trades over a decent
period of time.

619
01:24:01,000 --> 01:24:13,000
So the realities of this business and
running a trading account is that you not
only need to turn yourself into a trade
idea generation monster.

620
01:24:13,000 --> 01:24:26,000
You also need to turn yourself into a
banking beast.

621
01:24:26,000 --> 01:24:36,000
And when you combine the two being a trade
idea generation monster and being a really
effective risk manager, especially with
reactive risk management and becoming a
banking beast.

622
01:24:36,000 --> 01:24:45,000
This in your business, your trading
account leads to your realized P&L always
going up.

623
01:24:45,000 --> 01:24:52,000
And it means the cash register of your
business is ringing on a regular basis.

624
01:24:52,000 --> 01:25:01,000
Every time you're moving P&L from
unrealized to realized, you should just
always remember the ring of a cash
register.

625
01:25:01,000 --> 01:25:10,000
Are you taking the right action and being
up in the realized column on a net basis?

626
01:25:10,000 --> 01:25:17,000
Are you putting dollars into the register
or are you taking dollars out of the
register?

627
01:25:17,000 --> 01:25:29,000
You need to always keep the register
ringing and you have to make solid and
sensible decisions and play the
probabilities, not the possibilities.

628
01:25:29,000 --> 01:25:37,000
And just to hammer this point home, we
have here the visual of an actual cash
register in a business.

629
01:25:37,000 --> 01:25:41,000
And what's the cash register actually
asking you to do?

630
01:25:41,000 --> 01:25:50,000
The cash register is asking you to please
put money inside me and please don't take
it away.

631
01:25:50,000 --> 01:25:54,000
You are running a business here, guys.

632
01:25:54,000 --> 01:26:06,000
OK, your trading account is a business and
the actions that you take in your reactive
risk management is really going to
determine whether you're putting money
consistently into that register.

633
01:26:06,000 --> 01:26:12,000
Or you're consistently taking money out.

634
01:26:12,000 --> 01:26:15,000
Your realized column is your business.

635
01:26:15,000 --> 01:26:17,000
It's your cash register.

636
01:26:17,000 --> 01:26:23,000
Protect your realized column with your
life and keep it charging upwards.

637
01:26:23,000 --> 01:26:28,000
So, of course, reactive risk management,
staying in motion.

638
01:26:28,000 --> 01:26:31,000
It can't be taught in a video series.

639
01:26:31,000 --> 01:26:37,000
It has to be done with real experience,
with real money and real positions.

640
01:26:37,000 --> 01:26:48,000
However, in the PTM video series here,
what I've done in this video is try to set
you off on the right path in the context
of psychology.

641
01:26:48,000 --> 01:27:05,000
In terms of teaching reactive risk
management and doing this properly in
practice, it can really only be taught to
the professional level on a trading desk
or in a mentoring program with real money
through demonstration.

642
01:27:05,000 --> 01:27:11,000
We've done hundreds of mentoring programs
down the years at ITPM.

643
01:27:11,000 --> 01:27:16,000
And anyone who has done a mentoring
program at ITPM will actually know this.

644
01:27:16,000 --> 01:27:34,000
So, at this stage, if you haven't done a
mentoring program or you don't plan to,
then this, the reactive risk management
and staying in motion is a skill set that
you're going to have to learn for yourself
over a longer period of time.

645
01:27:34,000 --> 01:27:50,000
On a mentoring program at ITPM, you'll be
